{"name":"Sago Garden","hex":"#94be7f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sago","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A powdered starch obtained from certain palms (Metroxylon spp., esp. Metroxylon sagu), used as a flour and food thickener and for sizing textiles.","A similar starch obtained from palm-like cycad, especially Cycas revoluta."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of sego."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sago"},{"term":"garden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An outdoor area containing one or more types of plants, usually plants grown for food or ornamental purposes.","Such an ornamental place to which the public have access."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To grow plants in a garden; to create or maintain a garden.","Of a batsman, to inspect and tap the pitch lightly with the bat so as to smooth out small rough patches and irregularities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/garden"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sago","blocks":[{"text":"Sago is a starch extracted from the soft, spongy core tissue of several tropical palm species, especially Metroxylon sagu. For centuries, it has been an important traditional staple food among many indigenous communities across the islands of Southeast Asia and the Melanesian region, including Malaysia, Indonesia, and Papua New Guinea. In different regions, sago is also known by local names such as saksak, rabia, and sagu."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Sago_%28Metroxylon_sagu%29_in_New_Guinea.jpg/330px-Sago_%28Metroxylon_sagu%29_in_New_Guinea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sago"},{"title":"Garden","blocks":[{"text":"A garden is a planned space, usually outdoors, set aside for the cultivation, display, and enjoyment of plants and other forms of nature. The single feature identifying even the wildest wild garden is control. The garden can incorporate both natural and artificial materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Botanic_Garden_New_York_May_2015_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Metroxylon_sagu%2C_Sago_Preparation.jpg/500px-Metroxylon_sagu%2C_Sago_Preparation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Metroxylon_sagu%2C_Sago_Preparation.jpg/500px-Metroxylon_sagu%2C_Sago_Preparation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peeling and pounding a segment of sago palm stem to produce an edible starch. Sepik River, Papua New Guinea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMetroxylon_sagu%2C_Sago_Preparation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Sago_Palm_being_harvested_for_Sago_production_PNG.jpg/500px-Sago_Palm_being_harvested_for_Sago_production_PNG.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Sago_Palm_being_harvested_for_Sago_production_PNG.jpg/500px-Sago_Palm_being_harvested_for_Sago_production_PNG.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sago palm being harvested for sago production","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASago_Palm_being_harvested_for_Sago_production_PNG.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Sago_logs.jpg/500px-Sago_logs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Sago_logs.jpg/500px-Sago_logs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par